1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
The

2
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,022
invention of the wheel, if I may put it this way, invented a mind.

3
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:23,000
And in becoming inventors,

4
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,867
we begin to think of ourselves differently.

5
00:00:25,868 --> 00:00:29,000
Not simply imperiled by the facts of the world,

6
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,820
but perhaps competing with the facts of the world, as in a contest.

7
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:37,580
And a contest that we plausibly now might regard ourselves as winning.

8
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:45,180
The simple, elegant, yet revolutionary machine called the wheel has literally transformed mankind.

9
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:52,580
Not just in the way we navigate the challenges of our lives, but in the very way we perceive ourselves.

10
00:00:52,581 --> 00:00:53,281
ourselves.

11
00:00:53,282 --> 00:00:59,080
Quite a responsibility for a tool so ubiquitous that we encounter hundreds in a day

12
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:04,480
without even realizing it. So simple that we struggle to adequately define it.

13
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:13,325
I think a wheel is any circular object, regardless of its width, that rolls.

14
00:01:13,326 --> 00:01:15,560
And that means it might

15
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:21,940
have an axle to transmit its power to someplace else or to direct its power at a certain point.

16
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:26,580
Some say an axle is not a necessary component of a wheel.

17
00:01:26,581 --> 00:01:33,580
You can imagine wheels in which the mechanical wheel is not linked to that central axis, for example, in a ball bearing.

18
00:01:33,581 --> 00:01:36,537
A ball bearing goes around and makes a circular motion.

19
00:01:36,538 --> 00:01:38,580
It fulfills our definition of a wheel.

20
00:01:38,581 --> 00:01:41,580
But it doesn't have an axle.

21
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:45,580
The wheel and axle is one of six basic machines.

22
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:51,580
Human-made objects that are the basis of all devices that provide us with mechanical advantage over our environment.

23
00:01:52,580 --> 00:02:03,260
The other five include the lever, the inclined plane, the pulley, the wedge, and the screw.

24
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:10,480
You know, it's interesting how some of these machines, it becomes a vague distinction between them.

25
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,440
One of my favorites is a pizza cutter.

26
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:15,980
You've got a wheel, the edge is a wedge, and the handle is a lever.

27
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:21,260
So you're using a lever, a wedge, and a wheel to cut your pizza.

28
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:30,680
Mechanical advantage is important because it allows a single person with limited force to accomplish an otherwise impossible task.

29
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:35,980
Consider the wheeled machine known as a pulley.

30
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,980
Less force is required to achieve a given effect.

31
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:45,380
Now you have to apply the force over a much longer distance, and that's the key.

32
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:51,900
With a pulley, instead of just lifting up a 500-pound block, 10 feet, say, you might have to pull 100 feet of rope.

33
00:02:51,901 --> 00:02:56,140
But you don't have to apply as much force, and so eventually you get the job done.

34
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,260
That's mechanical advantage.

35
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:04,940
Some have speculated that the earliest wheels were logs, used to transport heavy loads.

36
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:10,920
I think it might be useful to make clear how little we know about that,

37
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:15,260
and to what extent we've made up stories.

38
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,100
They're pretty much made up stories.

39
00:03:17,101 --> 00:03:21,860
The archaeological evidence for the very first wheel is pretty slim.

40
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:26,900
Nevertheless, someone somewhere invented a wheel.

41
00:03:26,901 --> 00:03:32,900
And in doing so gained mechanical advantage because the wheel reduces friction.

42
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:37,900
Unlike a flat wide surface such as a placket of wood which generates a lot of friction,

43
00:03:37,901 --> 00:03:44,900
or a sharp narrow point, the wheel has a smooth, narrow and continuous surface.

44
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:50,900
It reduces friction because it makes contact on a small, narrow but endless plane.

45
00:03:50,901 --> 00:03:54,080
Plus, the wheel can store energy.

46
00:03:56,920 --> 00:03:59,880
Now, that's a very practical consequence of the invention,

47
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:02,340
but there's a much more profound consequence.

48
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:05,580
There's a cognitive consequence.

49
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:10,280
He is now reconsidering the very idea of limitation

50
00:04:10,281 --> 00:04:13,800
because he now has concrete evidence

51
00:04:13,801 --> 00:04:18,440
that what he once took to be unquestionably impossible

52
00:04:18,441 --> 00:04:22,580
is now not only possible, but practically achieved.

53
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:28,940
In the ancient world thousands of years ago,

54
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,540
three very important achievements of the wheel

55
00:04:31,541 --> 00:04:34,880
profoundly and dramatically changed the history of civilization.

56
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,160
First, the grinding stone,

57
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:41,360
which ground large quantities of grain more efficiently

58
00:04:41,361 --> 00:04:43,680
than an individual with a mortar and pestle.

59
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:49,080
And both millstones have a groove pattern cut into their faces.

60
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:54,040
The stone above it, not quite touching it, just a little bit above it, rotates.

61
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:57,260
The grain falls in the hole in the middle of the top stone.

62
00:04:57,660 --> 00:04:59,680
The grain works its way out along those grooves,

63
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:03,780
and as the top stone rotates, the grooves cross like scissors and shear apart the grain.

64
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:11,660
Entire communities could form around a single large grinding stone

65
00:05:11,661 --> 00:05:15,900
that might be powered by harnessing cattle rather than human power.

66
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,860
Grinding stones were carved out of large rocks,

67
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,160
then chiseled into round, flat, grooved discs.

68
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:29,880
Now that grain could be processed in such large quantities,

69
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:31,640
storage became a concern.

70
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,960
But the wheels solved this problem, too.

71
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:39,560
Potter's wheels, used for making clay pots,

72
00:05:39,561 --> 00:05:42,020
were initially created during the Bronze Age,

73
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,680
then later refined by the Egyptians roughly 5,000 years ago.

74
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:56,140
Potters could throw thin-walled pots with great storage capacity

75
00:05:56,141 --> 00:05:58,980
in a fraction of the time it took to make a pot without a wheel.

76
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,460
Pottery wheels are still used today.

77
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,880
With a kick wheel, you have the wheel head,

78
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,340
which is where the potter puts the clay.

79
00:06:08,341 --> 00:06:13,680
Below that, there's a shaft which is connected to a flywheel at the very base.

80
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:22,620
And the flywheel has quite a bit of weight to it so that the momentum will keep the wheel turning as the potter's kicking and working and alternating.

81
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:32,260
A flywheel is a wheel with added weight, usually placed at its circumference to store kinetic energy or the energy of motion.

82
00:06:32,261 --> 00:06:39,260
Basically what it means is the faster a mass is moving, the more energy it has.

83
00:06:39,261 --> 00:06:44,260
And you know, if you've got a wheel that's spinning right towards the center, things aren't moving very fast.

84
00:06:44,261 --> 00:06:47,260
But towards the outside, for the same amount of rotation, you're moving really fast.

85
00:06:47,261 --> 00:06:54,260
So if you put your mass on the outside of the flywheel, it's moving faster, it carries more kinetic energy,

86
00:06:54,261 --> 00:06:58,260
and therefore it's storing more energy for future use.

87
00:06:58,261 --> 00:07:04,580
The invention of the flywheel would have a wide-ranging impact far beyond the humble potter's wheel,

88
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:07,980
which would benefit from improvements that included the treadle.

89
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:13,260
In addition to the potter's wheel and the grinding stone,

90
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:18,540
the third application of the wheel, and possibly most significant, was for transportation.

91
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:24,680
Early log wheels evolved into narrow carved wheels that could be connected by an axle.

92
00:07:24,681 --> 00:07:29,840
An axle is a structure that is connected to, yet independent from, a wheel.

93
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,560
It is often found at the center of a wheel's rotation, or axis.

94
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:43,060
Wheeled carts helped transport goods, for instance, excess grain stored in clay pots.

95
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:49,300
The Sumerians provide the first archaeological evidence of wheeled vehicles.

96
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:54,220
Their chariots pulled by oxen date from as early as 3000 BC.

97
00:07:54,680 --> 00:08:00,760
Well, the craft of building wheels, especially in wagons and carriages, to me is very fascinating

98
00:08:00,761 --> 00:08:05,520
because the art of making the wheel, of course, ultimately creates a specialty called the

99
00:08:05,521 --> 00:08:06,520
wheelwright.

100
00:08:06,521 --> 00:08:12,600
Early wheelwrights made wheels by carving rims that would be clamped together with cross

101
00:08:12,601 --> 00:08:16,800
pieces with an axle fitted into the hub or center.

102
00:08:16,801 --> 00:08:22,080
A leather bearing might be inserted into the hub to protect the axle.

103
00:08:22,081 --> 00:08:29,800
The thing that's most useful to me, I think, in considering the history of the wheel, is that the wheel is only a part of a system.

104
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,320
A wheel's no good unless you have a good road for it to go on.

105
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:39,540
With the onset of carts, chariots, and wagons, roads took on greater importance.

106
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:43,780
Trade routes were established and civilizations could more readily expand.

107
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:47,980
But the wheel did much more than encourage the creation of carts and roads.

108
00:08:47,981 --> 00:08:54,660
It made migration easier and at relatively slight cost.

109
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,760
It meant that you could move without being nomadic.

110
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:03,200
You could bring along with you what in Roman times was called the impedimenta.

111
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:08,500
This was the spouse, the children, the relevant family possessions, etc.

112
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:12,760
It meant, therefore, that you could move your culture to another place.

113
00:09:12,761 --> 00:09:19,720
Before long, wheeled devices for transportation were available to farmers tilling the soil

114
00:09:19,721 --> 00:09:21,760
Traders transporting goods

115
00:09:21,761 --> 00:09:23,960
And armies transporting soldiers

116
00:09:23,961 --> 00:09:32,400
The Roman road would ultimately be based upon the 15-foot width of two heavy carts

117
00:09:32,401 --> 00:09:36,020
Or two columns of troops side by side

118
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,080
Once you've got that basic idea of a wheel

119
00:09:40,081 --> 00:09:45,200
then people are very quickly going to figure out all the fascinating applications

120
00:09:45,201 --> 00:09:48,200
whether two-wheeled carts pulled by other human beings

121
00:09:48,201 --> 00:09:52,040
or animals or wheelbarrows or chariots or whatever.

122
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:57,360
The wheel changed our sense of location

123
00:09:57,361 --> 00:10:01,640
our sense of rootedness.

124
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:07,200
It must have had a chilling effect once these implications dawned on us.

125
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:09,440
To some extent it still has a chilling effect.

126
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:17,340
It does give rise to the question, well, if I am not meant to be here, now, and abidingly,

127
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:22,660
is there any sense associated with my being meant to be?

128
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:25,220
Or is everything in flux?

129
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:35,940
The wheel really is the device that says no one is ever occupying precisely the same position twice once this thing starts rolling.

130
00:10:35,941 --> 00:10:42,140
Next, wheels rolled across the oceans as vital components of ships

131
00:10:42,141 --> 00:10:46,680
And long-distance trade made the importance of accurate timetables

132
00:10:46,681 --> 00:10:50,180
A new challenge that the promise of wheels would solve

133
00:10:50,181 --> 00:10:56,760
Although humans began settling in villages approximately 10,000 years ago

134
00:10:56,761 --> 00:11:01,740
It would be another 5,000 years before archaeological evidence of wheels would appear

135
00:11:01,741 --> 00:11:06,420
The Wheel will return on Modern Marvels

136
00:11:06,421 --> 00:11:14,760
During the late Middle Ages, as trade increased partly due to the application of wheels

137
00:11:14,761 --> 00:11:19,440
Merchants looked across the seas to new markets for buying and selling goods

138
00:11:19,441 --> 00:11:25,360
Large ships were built, using steering wheels to maneuver the rudder and direct the ship

139
00:11:25,361 --> 00:11:33,420
As the boat gets bigger, a tiller doesn't give you enough advantage, mechanical advantage, on the rudder.

140
00:11:33,421 --> 00:11:37,980
So you may hook up some gears and shafts or belts and pulleys and ropes,

141
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:43,640
but then you'll have a wheel because it's just so convenient to be able to stand in one spot and control the boat.

142
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:51,100
Cylindrical wheels called windlasses took up or let out rope to control the sails and anchor.

143
00:11:55,360 --> 00:12:02,300
The application of specific wheels often met specific challenges.

144
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:10,160
But the concept of wheels, wheelhood so to speak, seemed endlessly recyclable.

145
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:16,840
Perhaps the evolution, the changes, the inventions associated with the wheel

146
00:12:16,841 --> 00:12:21,560
somehow reflect something very fundamental about us as humans,

147
00:12:21,561 --> 00:12:25,840
about how our brains work, about how society evolves over time.

148
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:28,580
And the factors, the influences, the technologies

149
00:12:28,581 --> 00:12:31,980
that change who we are and what we do.

150
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:36,920
Not all cultures, however, have made use of wheels.

151
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:40,000
Despite its technological proficiency,

152
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:43,440
the Middle East eventually abandoned the wheel for transport

153
00:12:43,441 --> 00:12:45,920
because the camel better met its needs.

154
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:49,840
So the wheel is not the only solution to every problem.

155
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:53,800
Today we think the wheel is real important.

156
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:57,900
And it's important to remember that that's not always been true.

157
00:12:58,060 --> 00:13:03,700
The whole notion that the wheel and fire are the two most important inventions is a pretty recent idea.

158
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:08,020
Although the wheel is often not the only solution,

159
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:12,560
it is frequently a tangible framework for seemingly intangible challenges,

160
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:15,080
such as the regulation of time.

161
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:20,040
We think of the sun as a form of timekeeping in itself.

162
00:13:20,041 --> 00:13:25,200
rising in the morning and setting in the evening

163
00:13:25,201 --> 00:13:29,880
instantly gives a human being a sense of rhythm and form to time.

164
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:35,860
And so using the sun was an early means of timekeeping by using a sundial.

165
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,220
But sundials aren't precision instruments.

166
00:13:41,740 --> 00:13:43,920
As trade and travel increased,

167
00:13:43,921 --> 00:13:47,760
the need for accurate and reliable timetables increased with it.

168
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:52,500
Early clocks lacked the necessary accuracy.

169
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:59,380
What was needed and created around the 13th century was the modern wheelworks clock.

170
00:14:00,660 --> 00:14:08,420
Most people, when they think about a clock, immediately they look at the face of the clock with the hands and all the rest of it.

171
00:14:09,080 --> 00:14:13,860
That's almost, from my point of view, the wrong end of the clock.

172
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:18,300
If it's a pendulum clock that we're talking about, the pendulum, in fact, is the timekeeper.

173
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:30,860
Weights suspended from a fixed distance oscillate or swing at the same rate, no matter how heavy the weight nor how wide the swing.

174
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:39,360
Once the pendulum is swinging at a fixed rate, say 60 times per minute, the clock is keeping time.

175
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:48,400
A device called an escapement lowers a weight in discrete measures using specially designed wheels called gears.

176
00:14:50,040 --> 00:14:59,580
The gears help swing the pendulum and count each swing through the application of teeth carved into the gear to correspond with the teeth of another gear.

177
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:08,560
Gears are really nothing more than a set of wheels in some predetermined ratio.

178
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:12,640
And you put the gear teeth on to be sure that it doesn't slip, but they're wheels nonetheless.

179
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:18,480
These teeth turned the wheel into a series of levers extending from the hub.

180
00:15:19,180 --> 00:15:23,320
19th century observers even referred to the wheel as the perpetual lever.

181
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:32,320
In fact, Galileo, in analyzing the simple machines in the 1500s,

182
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:40,540
broke down all of the simple machines into a combination or a form of lever.

183
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:46,640
As clockmaking was refined, gears were used to create minute and second hands,

184
00:15:46,641 --> 00:15:49,380
greatly increasing the accuracy of the clocks.

185
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:53,440
Gears were made small to fit into wristwatches

186
00:15:53,441 --> 00:15:58,260
or large to operate clocks in church steeples.

187
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:05,220
They were even used to trigger chimes

188
00:16:05,221 --> 00:16:07,520
using a mallet connected to the gear system

189
00:16:07,521 --> 00:16:09,680
or gear train that struck a bell.

190
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:14,240
The principle of accurate timekeeping

191
00:16:14,241 --> 00:16:16,740
was applied on a large scale to help navigation.

192
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:21,140
The Earth was mapped out in a series of grids.

193
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:24,420
Each grid measured the distance the sun's light traveled

194
00:16:24,421 --> 00:16:27,300
across the surface of the Earth in four-minute increments,

195
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:28,480
or longitude.

196
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:31,780
With an accurate clock and a simple equation,

197
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:36,220
a sailor could now pinpoint his position anywhere on the planet.

198
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:42,200
Longitude only works if the Earth rotates in a wheel-like fashion.

199
00:16:42,201 --> 00:16:46,200
Now what would be a more perfect wheel?

200
00:16:46,201 --> 00:16:51,200
Well, one more perfect wheel is the wheel-like nature of the heavens themselves,

201
00:16:51,201 --> 00:16:55,200
where we see the axle, essentially a gravitational field,

202
00:16:55,201 --> 00:17:01,200
and where we see a wheel, now a planet, thus connected to other planets

203
00:17:01,201 --> 00:17:06,200
going around in a certain quasi-wheel-like fashion.

204
00:17:06,201 --> 00:17:12,300
And in fact, when astronomers wanted to make tiny models of the solar system,

205
00:17:12,301 --> 00:17:15,300
they used a series of wheels and a device called an ORI,

206
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:21,320
in which the planets were on wheels that moved around a central sphere, which represented the sun.

207
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:27,440
There's this wonderful progression from the physical reality of a wheel

208
00:17:27,441 --> 00:17:33,500
to the metaphor of a wheel in space as planets orbit the sun or as the moon orbits the earth.

209
00:17:33,501 --> 00:17:40,140
As wheels provide transport to different worlds, they transport themselves into different applications.

210
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:50,520
Technology incorporated the wheel into the printing press, facilitating mass-produced communication by pressing an inked plate onto paper attached to a roller.

211
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:56,740
Wheels were used to give the press constant pressure and to speed up the printing process.

212
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:02,240
Although the wheel wasn't essential to the production of books, it made the process more efficient.

213
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:09,780
What the book does is it puts thought in a form that allows it to be moved on wheels.

214
00:18:09,781 --> 00:18:14,280
It allows one to speak to the world as if the world were a person.

215
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:21,200
And it thus contains within it the possibility of revolutionizing the world without a drop of blood being shed.

216
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:27,820
Not to say that much blood will not be shed, and in fact much blood will be shed because of ideas that are presented in books.

217
00:18:27,821 --> 00:18:36,140
But it does mean that you now are capable of persuading without coercing, and in numbers.

218
00:18:38,340 --> 00:18:45,900
From the esoteric to the earthy, agrarian uses for wheels eventually included early windmills,

219
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:48,200
which arrived in Europe around the 12th century.

220
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:54,660
Windmills captured wind using large blades, often covered with cloth connected to a hub.

221
00:18:54,661 --> 00:19:01,660
The hub was attached to an axle that led to a series of gears.

222
00:19:01,661 --> 00:19:09,660
The windmill could pivot on wheeled rollers to adjust to changing wind directions.

223
00:19:09,661 --> 00:19:18,660
Windmills vary from traditional Dutch windmills to the familiar prairie windmills of the American plains.

224
00:19:18,661 --> 00:19:23,060
to the giant turbines that generate electricity on wind farms.

225
00:19:27,860 --> 00:19:34,540
Using gear trains, windmills pumped water out of wells, drained swamplands, and even ground wheat.

226
00:19:36,260 --> 00:19:42,380
As this 300-year-old Cape Cod windmill, the oldest in the country, once did for the pilgrims.

227
00:19:42,381 --> 00:19:53,040
what's interesting about the windmill however is that it harnesses naturally occurring energy

228
00:19:53,041 --> 00:19:58,560
rather than human or animal power one of the amazing things about wheels is the ability to

229
00:19:58,561 --> 00:20:04,340
take energy sources from around us just the environment and convert them to useful mechanical

230
00:20:04,341 --> 00:20:12,140
energy using a wheel next the concept of harnessing natural power would spark the industrial revolution

231
00:20:12,141 --> 00:20:14,980
and create even more ways to utilize wheels.

232
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:23,500
More than 100 million bicycles are manufactured worldwide each year,

233
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:27,980
with a bicycle being a primary mode of transportation in many developing countries.

234
00:20:29,120 --> 00:20:31,540
The wheel will return on Modern Marvels.

235
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:37,620
The mills of Europe, and later America,

236
00:20:37,621 --> 00:20:42,480
changed the natural environment into a resource to be exploited by the wheel.

237
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:47,600
Nature became another tool for human progress.

238
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:53,660
But the concept of harnessing wind and water actually dates back to the 2nd century B.C.

239
00:20:56,100 --> 00:21:01,360
Earliest uses that we know of are water lifting for irrigation

240
00:21:01,361 --> 00:21:05,060
and grinding grain, grinding wheat to flour.

241
00:21:05,061 --> 00:21:10,360
Water wheels harness water power in different ways.

242
00:21:11,440 --> 00:21:15,640
One is an impact water wheel, commonly called an undershot water wheel,

243
00:21:15,780 --> 00:21:20,540
where the water strikes a flat paddle and the impact causes the wheel to rotate.

244
00:21:21,300 --> 00:21:26,100
A second type of water wheel is called an overshot or a weight-driven water wheel,

245
00:21:26,240 --> 00:21:29,840
where the water comes over the top of the wheel, descends into a bucket,

246
00:21:29,841 --> 00:21:35,080
and the weight of the water in the buckets on one side of the wheel causes it to turn.

247
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:40,780
At the beginning of the 12th century, England had more than 5,000 mills,

248
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:45,440
each one a community power plant for a host of otherwise burdensome tasks

249
00:21:45,441 --> 00:21:48,440
far beyond just lifting water and grinding grain.

250
00:21:50,020 --> 00:21:56,280
European technicians and concurrently Chinese technicians diversify the use of water power

251
00:21:56,281 --> 00:22:02,200
to whereby 1500 they know how to use water power for pounding metals,

252
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:05,200
to shape metals for pounding ore prior to reduction,

253
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:08,940
for grinding a whole host of things other than wheat.

254
00:22:10,820 --> 00:22:14,140
Rivers, abundant in the northeastern United States,

255
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:18,060
would provide an accessible source of power during the early days of the nation.

256
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:24,660
There are a whole lot of rivers and streams that drop a fairly great distance,

257
00:22:24,661 --> 00:22:32,120
And the Industrial Revolution in America really takes off based on water power, particularly in the countryside.

258
00:22:33,660 --> 00:22:42,440
Villages and towns dug canals and built mills to saw wood, spin wool, and grind grain.

259
00:22:44,340 --> 00:22:49,520
On top of the millstones you have a hopper, a big wooden funnel that holds whole dried grain,

260
00:22:49,521 --> 00:22:52,700
be it wheat, rye, corn, barley.

261
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:57,380
And below that there's a shoe, a little wooden trough

262
00:22:57,381 --> 00:23:01,640
that directs the grain from the hopper into a pair of millstones.

263
00:23:02,340 --> 00:23:05,760
There's a vertical wooden shaft called a damsel

264
00:23:05,761 --> 00:23:09,240
that rotates with the millstone and shakes the shoe.

265
00:23:13,260 --> 00:23:16,640
This process is possible because the water wheel is connected

266
00:23:16,641 --> 00:23:19,460
through an elaborate system of gears and flywheels

267
00:23:19,461 --> 00:23:22,820
to the millstone, or whatever the mill was assigned to do.

268
00:23:25,100 --> 00:23:31,480
It represents a sort of quantum leap in the amount of energy that humans were able to extract.

269
00:23:31,980 --> 00:23:37,040
A human being can produce about one-tenth of a horsepower continuously over a six or

270
00:23:37,041 --> 00:23:37,741
eight-hour day.

271
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:45,300
That means even a small two-horsepower water wheel is producing 20 times as much energy

272
00:23:45,301 --> 00:23:48,620
as a human being, and it doesn't get tired.

273
00:23:49,460 --> 00:23:56,040
Water mills are an ingenious use of available resources,

274
00:23:56,480 --> 00:23:59,220
and by simply reversing the concept of the water wheel,

275
00:23:59,580 --> 00:24:02,140
you end up with something entirely different.

276
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:07,920
One of the neat things about wheels is the way you can turn this energy conversion around.

277
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:10,980
We have water wheels that produce energy of all sorts,

278
00:24:11,100 --> 00:24:13,720
whether it's electrical energy or energy for a grist mill,

279
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:17,260
but put it on a paddle boat, connect it to a steam engine,

280
00:24:17,261 --> 00:24:19,220
and you've got a paddle steamer.

281
00:24:19,460 --> 00:24:21,360
So it works both ways.

282
00:24:22,180 --> 00:24:27,620
The paddle wheel of a paddle steamer can be thought of as a series of ores attached to a wheel.

283
00:24:29,340 --> 00:24:33,820
Robert Fulton's paddle boats began flying the Mississippi in the early 19th century.

284
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:40,520
But the business end of a paddle steamer is really the steam engine that generates the energy to move the paddles.

285
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:48,020
And here again, the concept of the wheel manifests itself in an aptly named mechanism called the sun and planet gear.

286
00:24:49,460 --> 00:24:55,100
The planet is a small gear that orbits the sun, or gear connected by an axle to a driving wheel.

287
00:24:57,180 --> 00:25:03,160
As steam is forced into a piston chamber, it expands and moves a lever attached to the planet wheel,

288
00:25:03,660 --> 00:25:06,460
which is in turn connected to a spoke coming from the sun.

289
00:25:08,160 --> 00:25:10,480
The sun then moves the driving gear.

290
00:25:13,960 --> 00:25:18,880
The principle of the sun and planet gear is well illustrated on another transportation device

291
00:25:18,881 --> 00:25:22,720
that revolutionized the world, the steam locomotive.

292
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:29,260
At certain times in American mechanical history,

293
00:25:30,180 --> 00:25:35,760
rotary devices seemed to be the way to solve all problems.

294
00:25:35,761 --> 00:25:39,400
So for a while, in the 1840s in particular,

295
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:42,900
you have all these very, very clever rotary steam engines.

296
00:25:44,180 --> 00:25:48,340
Trains obliterated frontiers in America in the mid-19th century.

297
00:25:48,341 --> 00:25:51,100
The entire country was crisscrossed with track.

298
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:55,620
There were companies that manufactured car wheels,

299
00:25:55,800 --> 00:25:58,900
usually the axle and the two wheels to go into railway cars,

300
00:25:59,100 --> 00:26:02,260
but locomotive wheels would be built right along with the rest of the locomotives,

301
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:05,500
heavy castings, and then turned on a heavy lathe.

302
00:26:06,380 --> 00:26:09,600
And usually the wheel itself would be cast steel,

303
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:12,380
and then there'd be a forged steel tire,

304
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:15,220
and the tire would be expanded with a little heat,

305
00:26:15,221 --> 00:26:18,880
and then put in place and then allowed to cool and shrink down tight.

306
00:26:19,540 --> 00:26:22,900
With the advent of trains, wheat could be grown in Kansas,

307
00:26:23,460 --> 00:26:27,080
shipped to Chicago, and be ground, then packaged and distributed

308
00:26:27,081 --> 00:26:31,640
to New York or California, all mobilized on railroad wheels.

309
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:35,900
As railroads regulated the nation,

310
00:26:36,380 --> 00:26:38,460
they helped grease the Industrial Revolution,

311
00:26:38,900 --> 00:26:42,940
providing markets for goods more efficiently produced because of the wheel.

312
00:26:46,849 --> 00:26:57,139
The part of the wheel in the Industrial Revolution is central, not only as it influenced factory production, but in fact as it made possible the steam locomotive and a

313
00:26:57,140 --> 00:27:07,120
veritable empire that will depend on the movement of troops, the movement of people, the crossing of entire continents in periods of time heretofore unimaginable.

314
00:27:07,121 --> 00:27:14,120
The Industrial Revolution, in fact, rolls inexorably on wheels.

315
00:27:14,121 --> 00:27:23,120
Next, by the turn of the 20th century, this inexorable roll would generate even more wheel-based innovations.

316
00:27:23,121 --> 00:27:33,120
The number of locomotives in the United States peaked at nearly 70,000 in the early 1920s.

317
00:27:33,121 --> 00:27:36,120
The wheel will return on Modern Marvels.

318
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:45,120
As wheels harness natural power, they liberate us from otherwise exhausting or impossible tasks.

319
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:57,120
But the idea of the wheel as liberator took an ironic turn in places like Lowell, Massachusetts, during the mid-19th century.

320
00:27:57,121 --> 00:28:04,120
mid-nineteenth century, with its textile mills full of machines operated by young farm girls.

321
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:13,120
Textiles had for centuries used simple hand-operated spinning wheels to twist cotton, wool and

322
00:28:13,121 --> 00:28:14,413
flax into thread.

323
00:28:14,414 --> 00:28:20,120
But wheel-based innovations at the turn of the nineteenth century increased

324
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:22,120
production.

325
00:28:22,121 --> 00:28:25,240
The vertical spinners or jennies came in.

326
00:28:25,241 --> 00:28:30,180
Again, it was an effort to get more production with one worker.

327
00:28:30,720 --> 00:28:33,880
And so there are 10 spindles all run by one wheel.

328
00:28:36,120 --> 00:28:41,040
Eventually, single machines spun 200 spindles of wool at the same time.

329
00:28:42,060 --> 00:28:47,440
Planned communities like Lowell formed to produce textiles along rivers like the Concord and the Merrimack,

330
00:28:47,900 --> 00:28:51,620
which generated roughly 10,000 horsepower to Lowell's water wheels.

331
00:28:52,120 --> 00:28:58,980
It was an interesting social experiment to bring in the farm girls,

332
00:28:58,981 --> 00:29:05,660
and they thought they were being liberated from the hard work of the farms,

333
00:29:05,740 --> 00:29:09,920
only to discover that life in the mills could be long hours,

334
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:13,520
and that the hours were ruled not by the sun, but by the mill bell.

335
00:29:14,520 --> 00:29:20,980
What the Industrial Revolution did for women was that it rendered them to some extent independent

336
00:29:20,981 --> 00:29:24,720
of marriage.

337
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:28,140
It made self-support possible.

338
00:29:28,141 --> 00:29:33,380
But by creating an entirely unliberated world

339
00:29:33,381 --> 00:29:37,720
of incessant labor, of discomfort,

340
00:29:38,360 --> 00:29:42,420
and the conversion of oneself into a kind of instrument or tool.

341
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:45,600
A cog, if you will.

342
00:29:45,601 --> 00:29:53,580
The success of our machinery has convinced us, almost to a person,

343
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:57,840
that there is no problem so great that some machine can't solve it.

344
00:29:57,841 --> 00:30:03,740
And to that extent, we do tend to look for mechanical solutions to problems that are not mechanical.

345
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:11,780
But the inexorably spinning wheels of the Industrial Revolution produced an inexhaustible supply of products,

346
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:13,960
including more wheels.

347
00:30:15,600 --> 00:30:22,600
Despite our constantly reinventing the wheel, the concept of the wheel hasn't really evolved.

348
00:30:22,601 --> 00:30:25,600
A testimony to its simple purity.

349
00:30:25,601 --> 00:30:30,600
But in practice, wheels do evolve.

350
00:30:30,601 --> 00:30:33,600
They improve through technology and materials.

351
00:30:33,601 --> 00:30:36,600
The log evolved into the carved wheel,

352
00:30:36,601 --> 00:30:41,600
which became the lighter, more efficient hollowed rim with spokes and a hub.

353
00:30:41,601 --> 00:30:47,780
The tire evolved from a metal casing into something altogether transformative

354
00:30:47,781 --> 00:30:49,880
with the advent of the automobile.

355
00:30:51,640 --> 00:30:54,900
Because of speed and heaviness of a car,

356
00:30:55,160 --> 00:30:58,600
you've got to look differently at a vehicle that's going, say, 5, 10 miles an hour

357
00:30:58,601 --> 00:31:02,320
drawn by a horse on the rugged roads of the 19th century.

358
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:05,900
If you've got a car that's capable of 20 miles an hour,

359
00:31:06,360 --> 00:31:08,880
you've increased the stress on the wheels about four times,

360
00:31:09,500 --> 00:31:11,480
and your old wagon wheels begin to fall apart.

361
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:16,600
The automobile itself was another collection of gears.

362
00:31:16,601 --> 00:31:22,600
Crankshaft and steering wheel, ball bearing and transmission, all rolled out on an assembly line.

363
00:31:22,601 --> 00:31:25,600
Again, a variation on the wheel.

364
00:31:25,601 --> 00:31:31,600
But of all the wheels connected to the car, the greatest may be the pneumatic tire.

365
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:40,600
Tires were originally just cloth or leather coverings wrapped around a wood or metal wheel.

366
00:31:40,601 --> 00:31:45,589
wheel to provide cushioning. Eventually, hard rubber was used.

367
00:31:45,590 --> 00:31:47,600
Finally, pneumatic tires,

368
00:31:47,601 --> 00:31:52,600
which trapped a cushion of air inside the tire, were employed, initially on bicycles.

369
00:31:52,601 --> 00:31:59,600
But by the early 20th century, they found their way onto cars and trucks.

370
00:32:03,600 --> 00:32:09,600
Air is the most efficient thing that will carry the load and give you cushioning and

371
00:32:09,601 --> 00:32:14,600
you need certain footprint characteristics, force and moment characteristics for the tire,

372
00:32:15,100 --> 00:32:17,860
and the deflection of the tire will provide that

373
00:32:17,861 --> 00:32:23,920
and gives you very even distribution of load across the footprint with just air in the cavity.

374
00:32:26,360 --> 00:32:30,260
Before air could be pumped into a rubber tire, the rubber had to be hardened,

375
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:32,660
made impervious to changes in the weather.

376
00:32:33,760 --> 00:32:36,560
In 1839, Charles Goodyear, an inventor,

377
00:32:36,561 --> 00:32:41,860
searched for an additive that would keep rubber from turning brittle in the cold and sticky in the heat.

378
00:32:48,360 --> 00:32:50,740
He stumbled upon vulcanization,

379
00:32:51,220 --> 00:32:55,760
a curing process that basically cooks rubber until it stabilizes and holds its shape.

380
00:32:56,760 --> 00:33:00,140
So he had a lot of different chemicals that he was experimenting with,

381
00:33:00,360 --> 00:33:03,620
and he accidentally spilled some of the sulfur on the stove,

382
00:33:03,621 --> 00:33:08,100
and it, sulfur will melt under those conditions

383
00:33:08,101 --> 00:33:12,100
and he had some rubber laying on the stove and it ran together

384
00:33:12,101 --> 00:33:15,000
and it vulcanized, lo and behold.

385
00:33:15,760 --> 00:33:19,900
By the 1950s, synthetic rubber had replaced natural rubber.

386
00:33:20,320 --> 00:33:23,880
Tire companies like Goodyear really were reinventing the wheel.

387
00:33:24,780 --> 00:33:28,020
Again, the ideal wheel, the round thing that's frictionless,

388
00:33:28,220 --> 00:33:29,880
that's not something that gets better.

389
00:33:29,881 --> 00:33:36,520
But the practical wheel, the wheel that gives better mileage or breaks better or is lighter,

390
00:33:36,720 --> 00:33:43,680
that's the point where you can spend a lot of money and over the years get a lot of improvement in efficiency.

391
00:33:46,020 --> 00:33:50,100
Today, making tires is a complex and highly specialized endeavor.

392
00:33:50,980 --> 00:33:57,120
There are up to 15 different components in today's steel-belted radial tire.

393
00:33:57,121 --> 00:34:09,900
You have the beads, the plies, there's wedges, apexes, liner, sidewall, tread compound, sidewall compound, overlays.

394
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:18,840
There is some ongoing innovation work with tires with computer chips in them that will read the road, that will help control the vehicle.

395
00:34:18,841 --> 00:34:24,540
There will be an integral part of the vehicle control system, and they'll be reading it from inside the tire,

396
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:27,480
which is a great advantage since it's closer to the road.

397
00:34:29,420 --> 00:34:34,960
Goodyear manufactures tires for all sorts of vehicles, including off-road, earth-moving equipment,

398
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:40,600
farm vehicles, and airplanes.

399
00:34:41,680 --> 00:34:45,020
Airplane tires endure particular stress during takeoffs and landings

400
00:34:45,021 --> 00:34:48,020
because of heat caused by the friction from the weight of the cargo.

401
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:51,320
and the high speeds needed to achieve lift.

402
00:34:53,540 --> 00:34:57,840
Wheels play an indispensable role in the aeronautics industry beyond tires.

403
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:02,360
The propellers that first lifted the Wright brothers off the ground

404
00:35:02,361 --> 00:35:05,320
use the principle of the windmill, only in reverse.

405
00:35:06,780 --> 00:35:10,860
An axle from the center of the propeller connects through a complex gear system

406
00:35:10,861 --> 00:35:13,020
to an engine that supplies the spin.

407
00:35:14,020 --> 00:35:17,240
Instead of harnessing wind, a propeller generates it,

408
00:35:17,241 --> 00:35:19,040
lifting the plane off the ground.

409
00:35:22,980 --> 00:35:25,480
Switch the propeller from vertical to horizontal,

410
00:35:25,920 --> 00:35:28,900
and the wheel elevates the helicopter's rotary blades

411
00:35:28,901 --> 00:35:33,360
and elevates us from our earthbound existence,

412
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:37,040
free and unfettered, thanks to wheels.

413
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:41,600
I think there's a continuum here of mobility, if you will.

414
00:35:42,340 --> 00:35:44,000
And along with that mobility, I think,

415
00:35:44,001 --> 00:35:47,160
comes a lot of the basic freedoms that we, again,

416
00:35:47,240 --> 00:35:51,920
sometimes take for granted the right of free assembly depends on free

417
00:35:51,921 --> 00:35:57,140
transportation, free mobility, at least certainly transportation free of any

418
00:35:57,141 --> 00:36:03,480
political hindrance. Next, the impact of wheels extends far beyond the immediate

419
00:36:03,481 --> 00:36:07,640
benefits we reap. But are there other applications for wheels that we haven't

420
00:36:07,641 --> 00:36:13,840
even conceived of yet? Other materials in which to forge them? Other ways to

421
00:36:13,841 --> 00:36:14,840
experience them.

422
00:36:20,360 --> 00:36:27,000
A typical tire manufacturing plant can produce approximately 25,000 tires in a single day.

423
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:29,840
The wheel will return on Modern Marvelous.

424
00:36:30,900 --> 00:36:37,740
If the airplane propeller is a variation on the windmill, then the jet engine is a variation

425
00:36:37,741 --> 00:36:41,040
on the turbine, which was originally a type of water wheel.

426
00:36:41,041 --> 00:36:47,220
Water turbines are essentially water wheels with curved surfaces.

427
00:36:48,060 --> 00:36:54,040
They produce power by water flowing smoothly over a curved surface

428
00:36:54,041 --> 00:36:58,160
because the water acts over their entire surface simultaneously

429
00:36:58,161 --> 00:37:01,960
instead of on only one paddle or one bucket at a time.

430
00:37:02,100 --> 00:37:05,640
They produce much more power in a much more compact form.

431
00:37:05,641 --> 00:37:16,560
Hoover Dam makes use of over 17 14-foot-wide turbines to generate 2,991,000 horsepower,

432
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:19,760
which is then transformed into electricity.

433
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:24,400
Moving magnets create electricity.

434
00:37:25,420 --> 00:37:29,440
So in every turbine, you have coils of wire and you have magnets.

435
00:37:29,560 --> 00:37:32,480
And as you turn those coils of wire in the vicinity of a magnetic field,

436
00:37:33,100 --> 00:37:36,160
That's what produces the electricity that we use in our homes.

437
00:37:36,660 --> 00:37:42,020
Now you can generate that circular motion with a water-driven turbine, a water wheel.

438
00:37:42,580 --> 00:37:47,060
You can generate it with steam in a coal-burning power plant or a nuclear power plant.

439
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:48,820
You can generate it with wind.

440
00:37:52,580 --> 00:37:56,660
Jet engines replace water with air inside the spinning turbine.

441
00:37:57,320 --> 00:38:02,400
And instead of electricity, jet engines generate thrust as the air is pushed out of the engine.

442
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:03,580
lifting the plane.

443
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:07,780
From the circular motion of a spinning turbine

444
00:38:07,781 --> 00:38:10,800
to the spinning of a disc drive running a computer,

445
00:38:11,440 --> 00:38:12,960
we spin our wheels everywhere.

446
00:38:13,940 --> 00:38:16,460
The CD-ROM is similar to the compact disc,

447
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:19,720
which harkens back to the old vinyl record and turntable,

448
00:38:20,180 --> 00:38:22,720
which was a variation on the ancient hurdy-gurdy.

449
00:38:28,080 --> 00:38:31,180
In the future, we may need a microscope to see our wheels.

450
00:38:31,181 --> 00:38:37,380
One of the great frontiers of the wheels has to do with making them small, making them microscopically small.

451
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:38,180
This is nanotechnology.

452
00:38:39,200 --> 00:38:49,679
Right now, we can use lasers, we can use various other techniques to fabricate tiny pieces, including wheels and gears that are a hundredth the diameter of a human

453
00:38:49,680 --> 00:38:50,380
hair.

454
00:38:50,381 --> 00:38:56,540
You can make gear systems, gear trains on a surface that you need the highest power microscope just to see them.

455
00:38:56,541 --> 00:38:58,597
Now, these aren't machines yet.

456
00:38:58,598 --> 00:39:02,380
These are just demonstrating the feasibility of doing it.

457
00:39:02,381 --> 00:39:09,280
But imagine a society, imagine an age when we have micro-machines that can build other machines.

458
00:39:10,120 --> 00:39:12,940
Micro-machines that can do surgery on our bodies.

459
00:39:13,740 --> 00:39:19,960
Micro-machines that can fabricate micro-electronics, make your computers smaller, make them more powerful.

460
00:39:19,961 --> 00:39:27,060
wheels invaded every aspect of our lives with our undeniable utility and practicality

461
00:39:27,061 --> 00:39:33,060
and perhaps they've made us practical too but there's another wheel that's important to us

462
00:39:33,061 --> 00:39:39,340
a wheel built solely to amuse to talk about ferris wheels and balls and things like that

463
00:39:39,341 --> 00:39:47,200
as pointless mere games uh sort of life's trifles i think we grossly underestimate the power and

464
00:39:47,201 --> 00:39:56,520
force, the imaginative potential, the discipline of the mind wrought by such trivia as ferris wheels.

465
00:39:56,920 --> 00:40:01,960
After conquering so much of life's work with wheels, it's reassuring to acknowledge the

466
00:40:01,961 --> 00:40:05,629
importance of play and of the wheels' importance to play.

467
00:40:05,630 --> 00:40:08,140
Ferris wheels, despite their frivolity,

468
00:40:08,141 --> 00:40:13,315
are actually complex wheeled mechanisms requiring precision engineering.

469
00:40:13,316 --> 00:40:15,040
The largest ferris wheel

470
00:40:15,041 --> 00:40:17,120
in the world is the Millennium Wheel in London,

471
00:40:17,760 --> 00:40:19,560
opened in February of 2000,

472
00:40:19,561 --> 00:40:22,580
with a diameter of 443 feet.

473
00:40:25,400 --> 00:40:28,840
But the first true Ferris wheel was a giant as well,

474
00:40:29,340 --> 00:40:32,620
built for Chicago's Columbian Exposition of 1894

475
00:40:32,621 --> 00:40:36,140
by George Washington Gale Ferris,

476
00:40:36,660 --> 00:40:39,000
an American engineer who understood steel.

477
00:40:39,780 --> 00:40:42,060
It was first rejected out of hand.

478
00:40:42,180 --> 00:40:44,720
It's too wild and too improbable.

479
00:40:45,780 --> 00:40:48,000
So nothing else was forthcoming.

480
00:40:48,140 --> 00:40:50,920
So at the last minute, they got a hold of Mr. Ferris and said,

481
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:52,640
OK, we'll go with your idea.

482
00:40:54,240 --> 00:40:58,300
Ferris produced a 250-foot diameter steel wheel

483
00:40:58,301 --> 00:41:02,060
that rose 264 feet off the ground at its highest point.

484
00:41:02,900 --> 00:41:06,040
The giant axle was a single-forged piece of steel,

485
00:41:06,041 --> 00:41:09,060
the largest single-forging accomplished at that time.

486
00:41:10,080 --> 00:41:11,820
Two steam engines powered the wheel

487
00:41:11,821 --> 00:41:17,000
that carried over 2,000 passengers on a 40-minute journey through two revolutions.

488
00:41:18,080 --> 00:41:25,660
The first time you just got over your fear of sitting there hanging on to the seat,

489
00:41:25,840 --> 00:41:28,700
and the second time you got to look out and enjoy the ride.

490
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:35,100
One enthusiastic customer was Lee Sullivan's grandfather, William E. Sullivan,

491
00:41:35,101 --> 00:41:39,560
who talked the wheel's operator into letting him observe the mechanism that drove the wheel.

492
00:41:40,440 --> 00:41:44,620
When he came home, he told Grandma that that's what he wanted to do, build wheels.

493
00:41:45,680 --> 00:41:48,860
She said, well, don't tell anybody. They'll think you've lost your mind.

494
00:41:50,560 --> 00:41:55,740
Lee's grandfather turned his Jacksonville, Illinois bridge building business into a wheel making one.

495
00:41:57,020 --> 00:42:02,720
More than 100 years later, it continues as the oldest Ferris wheel manufacturer in the country.

496
00:42:02,721 --> 00:42:11,340
It's a long journey from the grinding stone to the ferris wheel

497
00:42:11,341 --> 00:42:18,660
But the story of wheels is an endless variation on a simple theme

498
00:42:18,661 --> 00:42:26,420
And so our inventions change us

499
00:42:26,421 --> 00:42:28,540
They don't just make us more powerful

500
00:42:28,541 --> 00:42:30,460
They make us think differently

501
00:42:30,461 --> 00:42:32,600
About the same sorts of things

502
00:42:32,601 --> 00:42:36,260
They make us think differently about the role of thought itself.

503
00:42:36,920 --> 00:42:39,860
You think about how much the wheel has driven society,

504
00:42:40,340 --> 00:42:44,540
how much it's increased a single human's ability to do more work,

505
00:42:45,020 --> 00:42:47,860
to get places that humans could never get before,

506
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:49,220
to travel greater distances,

507
00:42:49,860 --> 00:42:52,320
to do technologies you never could do before,

508
00:42:52,420 --> 00:42:54,820
just the ideas of things you could do with gear systems,

509
00:42:55,320 --> 00:42:56,600
things you could do with water wheels,

510
00:42:56,960 --> 00:42:58,300
things you could do with transportation.

511
00:42:59,420 --> 00:43:01,540
It's transformed society in amazing ways.

512
00:43:01,541 --> 00:43:04,920
It may not be the most important invention ever, but it's certainly right up there.

